<blockquote>
<p>put it below your autopsy part.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I am afraid I will reject this suggestion. I would rather go by chronology.</p>
<p>I <em>may</em> or <em>may not</em> consider reviewing the interview, but I personally regard it to be sufficient in length.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The word &quot;sensei&quot; seems really awkward in your interview log. If they were talking in English, why sensei?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I will note that I clearly stated that the interview was originally in Japanese. It is only presented here in English because this is an English site that uses English and we cater to English literates here. In my mind, the Foundation would have the original interview in Japanese and translate it into English. Some terms may be kept to prevent them from being lost in translation, which might change the nuances of the statements entirely.</p>
<p>I use &quot;sensei&quot; as it is a conventional term to term to a priest (at least from Google). It is mostly like calling a Protestant priest &quot;Father&quot;. I could translate it as &quot;Father&quot; or &quot;priest&quot;, but that would feel utterly awkward and imply Christianity instead (which is incorrect).</p>
<p>Mind you that &quot;sensei&quot; is not necessarily &quot;teacher&quot;, but it is a broader term for people worthy of some form of respect in society. In English, we don't really have a precise word to express that concept (or at least something that is concise enough to be spoken in conversation).</p>

							</div>

								
